  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nSynoptically, on Sunday morning an upper level trough looks to \nbegin moving through the Tri-State Area, potentially with an \nassociated weak jet streak. Come Monday, the trough begins to \nmove eastward and out of the area. Our upper level flow should \nthen return to come from the west until around Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday morning.\n\nHigh temperatures for the long term look to remain cooler than \nprevious days, with highs mainly in the 70s. The warmest day of \nthe period looks to be Wednesday with highs in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s for the majority of the CWA. Winds are forecast to be \nlight and variable throughout the late weekend into the early \nwork week. During this period, there are chances for the \nmajority of the area to see typical afternoon showers/storms. \nSunday currently looks to have the greatest chance of seeing \nprecipitation. NBM guidance shows around a 30-60% chance of \nexceeding 0.1 inches of precip from the early morning, and \ndecreasing in the afternoon. Monday through Wednesday the \nProbability of Precipitation is around 20-40% each afternoon. \nGuidance indicates lower values of CAPE and moderate shear, \nwhich would lean more towards showers than severe storms. \n\nBeginning Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, uncertainty in \nthe forecast begins to increase. For Tuesday night, guidance is \nhinting at troughing in the Western CONUS. By Wednesday morning,\nan upper level ridge is expected to be located over the \nSouthern US / Gulf region, with signs of a weak trough moving \nthrough the Northern Plains. The primary uncertainty lies in how\nstrong the high will be, where its center will locate, and \nwhether the weak trough to the west moves quickly, slowly, or \nremains nearly stationary. Guidance indicates the center of the \nridge could be as far north as the AR/MO/OK border or as far \nsouth as the Gulf. Certain models show the trough sweeping \nthrough in a single day, while others keep it near stationary \nover the AZ/CA/NV border. The exact locations of the ridge and \ntrough will impact our precipitation chances for Wednesday. It \nwill be interesting to see how guidance handles this upper level\npattern uncertainty as we get closer.",
